\[\langle\overrightarrow{k}^{2}_{T}\rangle=\langle\overrightarrow{k}^{2}_{T} \rangle_{vac}+2\left(\frac{\mu^{2}}{\lambda}\right)_{eff}\langle L\rangle_{ IS}+2\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)_{projection}\left(\frac{\mu^{2}}{\lambda} \right)_{eff}\langle L\rangle_{FS} \tag{7.5}\]

where a typical range for the transport coefficients for gluons in cold nuclear matter is given by \((\mu^{2}/\lambda_{g})_{eff,\ IS\approx FS}=\) 0.1-0.3 GeV\({}^{2}\)/fm. The value of \(\langle\overrightarrow{k}^{2}_{T}\rangle_{vac}=\) 1.5 GeV\({}^{2}\)/c\({}^{2}\) is extracted from a di-hadron correlation analysis of preliminary p+p data from PHENIX. A similar analysis using the p+p data from STAR presented in this thesis yields \(\langle\overrightarrow{k}^{2}_{T}\rangle_{vac}=\) 1.7 GeV\({}^{2}\)/c\({}^{2}\).7 No uncertainties are available for these \(k_{T}\) values. Fig. 7.3 shows a comparison of Vitev's resulting perturbative calculation with the STAR p+p data. The data show reasonable quantitative agreement, with the slight discrepancy at \(2.3<\Delta\phi<2.5\) attributed to the assumption of a gaussian \(k_{T}\) distribution. Power law corrections to this approximation are small and can be calculated [68][15]. Vitev and Qiu then calculate the additional \(p_{\perp}\) broadening in cold nuclear matter (d+Au collisions) using Eq. 7.5. Fig. 7.4a shows comparison of data and theory for p+p and d+Au data from STAR. The d+Au data come from RHIC RunIII and are not a part of this thesis analysis8 but they are a critical constraint of modifications due to cold nuclear matter. The Vitev-Qiu prediction is in excellent agreement with the away-side correlation peak in the d+Au data.

