## Appendix E The Optical Glauber Model

In the optical approximation, we can write analytic formulae for the AA collision. Our goal is to write an analytic formulation for the total cross section. To begin, we choose to work in cylindrical coordinates, as shown in Figure E.3.

We next introducethe individual thickness function

\[T_{A}(\vec{s})=\int p_{A}(\vec{s},z_{A})\ dz_{A}\] (E.5)

which represents the properly normalized density element at a given location \(\vec{b_{A}}\) in nucleus \(A\) integrated over all \(z\). We can write the same expression for nucleus \(B\), as well. Folding these two probability distributions together, we arrive at what is the overall thickness function

\[T_{AB}(b)=\int d^{2}s\ T_{A}(\vec{s})\ T_{B}(\vec{s}-\vec{b})\] (E.6)

where it is important to remember that the integrand is in cylindrical coordinates. The thickness function represents the probability (normalized to 1) to find a nucleon from \(both\) nucleus \(A\) and nucleus \(B\) at a given location. The probability for these two nucleons to interact is given by the non-singly diffractive nn cross section

\[\sigma_{in}=42\ (41)\ mb\] (E.7)

at \(\sqrt{s}\)= 200 (130) \(\rm GeV\). We can then use binomial statistics to write down the probability of \(n\) inelastic nn interactions at an impact parameter \(\vec{b}\) as

\[P(n,\vec{b})=\left(\begin{array}{c}AB\\ n\end{array}\right)[T(b)\sigma_{in}^{n}][1-T(b)\sigma_{in}]^{AB-n}\] (E.8)

where the first term represents the number of possible configurations yielding \(n\) interactions given \(A\cdot B\) possible nn encounters, the second term represents the probability of \(n\) collisions, and the third term represents the probability of exactly \(A\cdot B-n\) misses. We then arrive at an expression for the total probability of an AA interaction between nuclei \(A\) and \(B\) by summing equation E.8

\[\frac{d\sigma_{in}^{AB}}{d\vec{b}}=\sum_{n=1}^{n=AB}P(n,\vec{b})=1-1[1-T(b) \sigma_{in}]^{AB}\] (E.9)

In figure E.4 we plot equation E.9. Finally, we can integrate equation E.9 to obtain the total AA cross section

\[\sigma_{in}^{AB}=\int d\vec{b}\{1-[1-T(b)\sigma_{in}]^{AB}\}\] (E.10)One must remember that the integral is in cylindrical coordinates, explicitly

\[\sigma^{AB}_{in}=\int_{0}^{\infty}b\ db\{1-[1-T(b)\sigma_{in}]^{AB}\}\] (E.11)

It is worthwhile to mention that equation E.10 represents the best estimate of the (yet unmeasured) Au+Au cross section at RHIC.

We can go yet farther and calculated two important geometrical quantities. First, we can establish the total number of billiard ball like nn collisions (\(N_{coll}\)) via

\[\langle N_{coll}(b)\rangle=\sum_{n=1}^{n=AB}n\ P(n,b)=A\cdot B\cdot T(b)\sigma_ {in}\] (E.12)

Second, we can establish the total number of incoming nucleons that participated in \(at\ least\ one\) inelastic collision (\(N_{participant}\) or \(N_{part}\))

\[\langle N_{part}(b)\rangle=2A\int d^{2}s\ T_{AA}(\vec{s})\{1-(1-T_{AA}(\vec{s}- \vec{b})\sigma_{in})^{A^{2}}\}\] (E.13)

where we have set \(A=B\) for simplicity, although equation E.13 can of course be generalized for \(A\neq B\).

To relate \(\langle N_{coll}\rangle\) and \(\langle N_{part}\rangle\) to an experimental observable, e.g. \(dN/dN_{ch}\), the

